  "content": {
    "id": "p52-g100-103",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Find the value(s) of $k$ for the following to have two distinct real roots.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p52-g100-103-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$x^2+x+k=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k<\\frac14$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta &= 1^2-4(1)(k) \\\\ \\Delta &= 1-4k\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor two distinct real roots, $\\Delta>0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}1-4k &> 0 \\\\ k &< \\frac14\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p52-g100-103-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$kx^2+2x+10=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k<\\frac1{10}$ and $k\\ne0$.\n\nUse $\\Delta=4-40k>0$ and exclude the linear case $k=0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta &= 2^2-4(k)(10) \\\\ \\Delta &= 4-40k\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor two distinct real roots, $\\Delta>0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}4-40k &> 0 \\\\ k &< \\frac1{10}\\end{align*}$$\n\nAt $k=0$, the equation becomes $2x+10=0$, which has only one solution. Hence $k<\\frac1{10}$ and $k\\ne0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p52-g100-103-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$kx^2-(2k-1)x+k=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k<\\frac14$ and $k\\ne0$.\n\nUse $\\Delta=1-4k>0$ and exclude the linear case $k=0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta &= [-(2k-1)]^2-4(k)(k) \\\\ \\Delta &= 4k^2-4k+1-4k^2 \\\\ \\Delta &= 1-4k\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor two distinct real roots, $\\Delta>0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}1-4k &> 0 \\\\ k &< \\frac14\\end{align*}$$\n\nAt $k=0$, the equation becomes $x=0$, which has only one solution. Hence $k<\\frac14$ and $k\\ne0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p52-g100-103-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$4(k+1)x^2+4kx+(k-2)=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k>-2$ and $k\\ne-1$.\n\nUse $\\Delta=16(k+2)>0$ and exclude the linear case $k=-1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta &= (4k)^2-4[4(k+1)](k-2) \\\\ \\Delta &= 16k^2-16(k^2-k-2) \\\\ \\Delta &= 16(k+2)\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor two distinct real roots, $\\Delta>0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}16(k+2) &> 0 \\\\ k &> -2\\end{align*}$$\nAt $k=-1$, the equation becomes $-4x-3=0$, which has only one solution. Hence $k>-2$ and $k\\ne-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      }
    ]
  },
